        "---\n",
        "## 3. Hypotheses and Statistical Tests\n",
        "**Goal:** test how strongly each predictor is related to the outcome, and whether groups differ."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "AwKM9ckJSxmZ"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (hypotheses for the correlation tests):**\n",
        "For **each** predictor I test the same pair of hypotheses:\n",
        "- **Null hypothesis (H0):** there is no linear relationship between the predictor and the outcome (the true correlation is 0).\n",
        "- **Alternative hypothesis (H1):** there is a linear relationship (the true correlation is not 0).\n",
        "\n",
        "I use **Pearson's r** (strength of a straight-line relationship) with **Spearman's rho** (strength of a rank relationship that does not assume a straight line) as a robustness check. I test 19 predictors at once, so I use a Bonferroni-adjusted cutoff of 0.05 / 19 to avoid calling something significant by chance. With 735 tracts almost every relationship will be statistically significant, so I focus on the **size** of each correlation and not only on the p-value."
      ]
    },
