      "source": [
        "**Write-up note (reading the correlation table):**\n",
        "- **Strongest relationships:** share_black2010 (r = -0.77), hhinc_mean2000 (r = 0.70), singleparent_share2010 (r = -0.68), share_asian2010 (r = 0.66). Neighborhoods with higher shares of Black residents, higher single-parent shares, and higher poverty tend to have lower income ranks for low-income children; neighborhoods with higher past household income and more college graduates tend to have higher ranks.\n",
        "- **A caution on the math score:** `gsmn_math_g3_2013` looks strong (r = 0.72) but it is a county-level value repeated for every tract, so it mostly separates counties from each other. I leave it out of the list above and handle it in Section 6.\n",
        "- **Weakest relationships:** job_density_2013 (r = 0.02), share_hisp2010 (r = 0.09), traveltime15_2010 (r = 0.22). For job density the p-value (0.64) is large, so I **fail to reject** H0: I do not find evidence of a straight-line relationship.\n",
        "- **Pearson and Spearman agree** on direction for almost every predictor. They differ most for the Hispanic share (Pearson 0.09, Spearman 0.27), which signals a curved or uneven relationship, so I treat that predictor with caution.\n",
        "- 17 of the 19 predictors pass the Bonferroni cutoff.\n",
        "- **Caution:** these are associations, not causes. Neighborhood demographics reflect a long history of segregation and unequal investment, so a strong race-share correlation says something about the structure of opportunity across places and not about the people who live there."
      ]
    },
