        "**Write-up note (do the relationships hold inside each state?):**\n",
        "Yes, the direction holds in every state. The strongest predictors keep the same sign inside DC, Maryland, and Virginia separately, so the pattern is not only a difference between states. How strong the relationship is changes by place: in DC the correlations stay close to the all-tract values (for example, mean household income 2000 has r = 0.76 in DC), while in Maryland and Virginia they are noticeably weaker. The college share drops the most in Virginia (r = 0.22), against 0.71 in DC. This tells me part of the overall relationship comes from differences between states and counties, so I include state in the feature set to keep the regional difference from being credited to the neighborhood characteristics."
