        "---\n",
        "## 6. Feature Selection and Engineering\n",
        "**Goal:** decide which predictors to keep and prepare them: handle overlapping columns, transform skewed columns, and encode categories."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {
        "id": "2Gi_iIbvSxma"
      },
      "source": [
        "### 6.1 Remove overlapping and unsuitable predictors\n",
        "\n",
        "**Write-up note (decisions on which predictors to drop):**\n",
        "I make each decision using the evidence from Sections 3 and 4:\n",
        "- **Total jobs within 5 miles:** correlated with high-pay jobs at about r = 1.00. I keep the high-pay jobs column, which has a slightly stronger relationship with the outcome.\n",
        "- **Median household income 2016:** correlated with mean household income 2000 at r = 0.88. I keep the 2000 measure because it is a stronger predictor and describes the neighborhood **during** the children's childhood, which fits the project question better than a 2016 measure taken after the outcome was measured. The 2016 column also has a Census top-code (250,001) that I found during wrangling.\n",
        "- **Share White:** correlated with share Black at r = -0.87 and with college share at r = 0.87. I keep share Black (the stronger predictor) and the college share (a different idea: education), and drop share White.\n",
        "- **Third-grade math score:** the check below shows it is constant within each county, so it cannot explain differences between tracts. It would act as a county label and overlap with the state variable, so I drop it."
      ]
    },
    {
