      "source": [
        "### 6.2 Transform skewed predictors\n",
        "\n",
        "**Write-up note (log transforms):**\n",
        "Linear models and correlations work best when predictors are not dominated by a few extreme values. I apply a log transform (`log1p`, which is safe for zeros) to a kept predictor only when two things are true: its skew is above 1, **and** the log brings the skew back under 1. In my data that is popdensity2010, job_density_2013. These extreme values are real dense tracts, so transforming keeps all of the tracts while reducing the influence of the extremes. A few share-type columns (poverty, Hispanic, Asian, and short-commute shares) are also skewed, but the log does not fix them (their skew stays above 1) and they are bounded between 0 and 1, so I leave them as they are. The before-and-after plots below show the effect."
      ]
