        "**Write-up note (summary of findings):**\n",
        "\n",
        "**1. Which variables best explain the project question?**\n",
        "The strongest relationships with the income rank of low-income children are share_black2010 (r = -0.77), hhinc_mean2000 (r = 0.70), singleparent_share2010 (r = -0.68), share_asian2010 (r = 0.66). Education and past income are positively related to outcomes; single-parent share, poverty, and the share of Black residents are negatively related. Job density and the Hispanic share are weak. Because these are associations in neighborhood-level data, they describe how opportunity is distributed across places and not what causes outcomes for any one child.\n",
        "\n",
        "**2. Do subgroups differ?**\n",
        "Yes. Average income rank is 0.37 in DC, 0.40 in Maryland, and 0.51 in Virginia. ANOVA, Kruskal-Wallis, and Tukey's HSD all show the states differ, and state explains about 54% of the variation. The main predictors keep their direction within each state, but they are noticeably weaker inside Maryland and Virginia than across all tracts, so I include state as a predictor.\n",
        "\n",
        "**3. Which variables correlate strongly with each other?**\n",
        "Four pairs are above 0.8: total jobs and high-pay jobs, median household income 2016 and mean household income 2000, college share and share White, and share White and share Black. I dropped one column from each overlapping set, and I dropped the math score because it is constant within each county.\n",
        "\n",
        "**4. Which tests fit?**\n",
        "Pearson correlation for linear relationships, with Spearman as a robustness check for skewed predictors. One-way ANOVA for comparing the three states and five counties, with Kruskal-Wallis as a check that does not assume normality and Tukey's HSD for pairwise differences. A Welch t-test compared DC with the rest of the region. I used a Bonferroni cutoff because I tested 19 predictors at once, and I reported effect sizes because with 735 tracts almost everything is statistically significant.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Limitations to mention to my mentor:** the outcomes are for children born in 1978-1983, so the predictors describe neighborhoods in 2000 to 2015 and not the exact childhood years; the outcome estimates are noisier for tracts with few children; and these are associations at the tract level, not cause and effect.\n",
